WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:07.073
Sequences put fungi closer to animals than to plants.

2
00:00:08.293 --> 00:00:09.853
Yet a fungus eats like neither.

3
00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:14.473
So how does a mould on bread eat, with no mouth and no gut?

4
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:17.653
Watch a spore land on the bread.

5
00:00:18.833 --> 00:00:21.213
It grows a single thread, called a hypha.

6
00:00:22.373 --> 00:00:26.333
The hypha branches again and again into a mass called a mycelium.

7
00:00:27.453 --> 00:00:28.793
That fuzz is the mould.

8
00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:35.923
Each thread is wrapped in a wall that contains chitin, a tough polymer also found in

9
00:00:35.923 --> 00:00:37.343
insect exoskeletons.

10
00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:40.283
Fungi do not photosynthesize.

11
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:43.723
So this mould's food must come from the bread.

12
00:00:44.767 --> 00:00:48.507
At the growing tip, the hypha releases enzymes into the bread.

13
00:00:49.807 --> 00:00:54.967
The enzymes cut starch, a large molecule, into small sugars outside the fungus.

14
00:00:56.387 --> 00:00:58.207
Then the hypha takes the sugars in.

15
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:02.767
So a fungus digests first and takes food in second.

16
00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:05.587
Most animals do the reverse.

17
00:01:06.847 --> 00:01:09.347
They take food into a gut, and digest it there.

18
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:13.507
A thin thread suits this way of eating.

19
00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:19.407
Every part of its cytoplasm stays close to the surface where the food comes in.

20
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:22.297
Some fungi skip the threads.

21
00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:25.017
A yeast is a single cell.

22
00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:30.997
Baker's yeast reproduces by budding, pinching a small daughter cell off a larger

23
00:01:30.997 --> 00:01:31.997
one.

24
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.120
Now, how many fungi mate.

25
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:39.980
Two hyphae of different mating types grow toward each other and meet.

26
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.460
Their cytoplasm joins.

27
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.380
This step is called plasmogamy.

28
00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:50.803
Each partner brought a haploid nucleus, carrying one set of chromosomes.

29
00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:56.943
Now the joined cell holds both nuclei, one from each partner, side by side.

30
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.563
Two sets of chromosomes, in one cell.

31
00:02:01.763 --> 00:02:03.063
So is this cell diploid?

32
00:02:04.223 --> 00:02:05.703
Think about where the two sets are.

33
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.400
Not yet. A diploid nucleus holds both sets in one nucleus.

34
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.060
Here the two sets sit in two separate nuclei.

35
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.280
A cell like this is called a dikaryon.

36
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.080
In mushroom-forming fungi, this stage can last a long time.

37
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:35.163
Only a later step fuses the two nuclei into one diploid nucleus.

38
00:02:36.463 --> 00:02:37.823
That step is karyogamy.

39
00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:40.423
Plasmogamy joins cells.

40
00:02:41.903 --> 00:02:43.403
Karyogamy joins nuclei.

41
00:02:44.333 --> 00:02:48.453
Then meiosis follows, and the cycle comes back to where it began.

42
00:02:49.713 --> 00:02:53.773
It makes haploid spores, each carrying one set of chromosomes again.

43
00:02:55.467 --> 00:03:00.327
So a fungus eats by digesting outside itself and absorbing what it frees.

44
00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:02.847
And it mates in two steps.

45
00:03:04.387 --> 00:03:06.187
Cells fuse first, and nuclei later.
